  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSatellite and radar trends at the time of writing place the \nbulk of the forecast area within clear skies and quiet weather\nafter the slow departure of overnight/early morning convective\ncomplexes that rolled through. However, will have to monitor\ndeveloping cumulus fields across central and eastern Kentucky,\nas this will provide the next round of showers and storms slated\nfor later this evening.\n\nHi-res simulated reflectivity models show another signal of\novernight shower and thunderstorm potential setting up\nadditional flash flooding concerns heading into Tuesday morning.\nThe QPF forecast shifts the attention off of an already \nbattered southeast Ohio east of the river to mainly West\nVirginia. Scattered showers and storms are progged to form late\nin the night in similar fashion to last night, stirring up\novernight flash flooding issues. Locally higher amounts of rain\ncould yield the use of amplified flash flood warning messaging,\nso ensure you have ways of receiving a warning that could be\nissued overnight.\n\nFlash flooding concerns, while still dependent on where the \naxis of heaviest rainfall sets up over the next few days, will \nbe highest across northeast WV and down the spine of the \nAppalachians. An inverted trough feature setting up over this \nregion, in tandem with a coastal low riding up along the eastern\nseaboard, will promote episodic rounds of showers and storms \nthrough midweek. This could set the stage for one to two inches \nof additional rainfall to arise. With that in mind, opted to \nextend the Flood Watch through Wednesday morning as we finish \nthe last few days of active weather before a period of dry \nweather reaches the Central Appalachians.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe upper level low that has remained trapped overhead will\nfinally track offshore heading into the end of the work week.\nStrong subsidence and northwest flow prompted by a building\nridge will help to evacuate any lingering moisture over the\nregion. Quiet and pleasant fall-like weather enters the forecast\nfor most on Thursday, outside of a rogue shower or storm still\nhovering over the higher terrain. Daytime temperature spread of\nthe 60s to low 70s will topple down to cool, crisp overnight\nlows spanning the upper 30s to upper 40s. While clouds will be\nslow to lift and erode on Thursday, ample sunshine by Friday\nwill lead the charge to what is looking to be a Chamber of\nCommerce weather weekend.",
